<UserControl xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             xmlns:cards="using:TanMenu.Wpf.Views.Settings"
             x:Class="TanMenu.Wpf.Views.SettingsPage">

  <!-- Windows 11 Settings' shape: one scrolling column of grouped cards, no tabs and no
       OK/Cancel/Apply. Every control writes through to config the moment it changes. Every card group
       below implements ISettingsCard — SettingsPage finds and reloads every ISettingsCard among
       CardStack's children itself (see ApplyStaticText in the code-behind), so adding a new group here
       is the only step needed; there is no separate call to remember.

       The 24/16/24/24 inset is a MARGIN on CardStack, not a Padding on the ScrollViewer — it used to be
       the latter, and that made the bottom 2×Padding.Top + Padding.Bottom worth of content (56 DIP here:
       2×16 + 24) permanently unreachable at VerticalScrollPercent=100 (confirmed live via UI Automation's
       ScrollPattern: 检查更新's bottom edge sat measurably below the window's own bottom edge — a real
       defect, not the PrintWindow capture artifact Round 1's review wrongly guessed). Root cause,
       confirmed by decompiling Avalonia.Controls 12.1.0: ScrollContentPresenter overrides
       MeasureOverride/ArrangeOverride and — unlike plain ContentPresenter — never deflates by its own
       Padding during Measure; Padding is applied ONLY at Arrange time, via Rect.Deflate on the CHILD's
       arranged Bounds. ComputeExtent then reads that already-shrunk Bounds.Size (full height minus
       Padding.Top AND Padding.Bottom) as the scrollable Extent, while StackPanel.ArrangeOverride
       (confirmed in its own source) positions children at their full, un-shrunk stacked height,
       OFFSET DOWN by Padding.Top again (Deflate insets the arranged rect's origin, not just its size) —
       so the true rendered bottom sits at Padding.Top + fullHeight, while the Extent only ever reports
       fullHeight − Padding.Top − Padding.Bottom as scrollable. The gap between the two — what 100% scroll
       still cannot reach — is exactly (Padding.Top + fullHeight) − (fullHeight − Padding.Top − Padding.Bottom)
       = 2×Padding.Top + Padding.Bottom, not Padding.Top + Padding.Bottom as first estimated: the top
       padding costs twice, once shrinking the reported Extent and once pushing the real content down.
       Margin does not have this bug: every control's own Measure/Arrange resolves it generically, and
       ComputeExtent explicitly inflates the child's Bounds by ITS Margin — so CardStack's true full
       height, insets included, is exactly what ends up in the Extent. -->

  <!-- x:Name is intentional even though no code references it: it is what gives this ScrollViewer a
       stable UI Automation AutomationId, which is how the "the last card is reachable at 100% scroll"
       check above is actually measured (ScrollPattern.VerticalScrollPercent on a named element, rather
       than hunting for an anonymous scroll region by tree position). Keep it. -->
  <!-- Round 3, Task 3 (user item 7): cards used to sit at a fixed MaxWidth="720" with
       HorizontalAlignment="Left" — a shrink-wrapped column parked against the left edge no matter how
       wide the window got. Task Manager's own settings cards (measured live) stretch
       to fill the content area with a ~24-25 DIP gutter on both sides up to a measured ~1250 DIP cap,
       then centre once that cap is hit. StackPanel's default HorizontalAlignment is already Stretch
       (only the explicit "Left" above was overriding it), and this ScrollViewer never turns on
       HorizontalScrollBarVisibility (it only sets Vertical), so ScrollContentPresenter's own
       MeasureOverride gives CardStack the real, bounded viewport width rather than
       PositiveInfinity — confirmed against Avalonia 12.1.0's own decompiled source, not assumed — which
       is what lets Stretch mean something here instead of silently doing nothing the way it would if
       horizontal scrolling were enabled. Layoutable.ArrangeCore's own Stretch case centres a
       MaxWidth-clamped child in exactly the leftover space this needs, for free, once the cap engages.
       Margin stays 24,16,24,24 — Task Manager's own measured gutter already matched that value. -->
  <ScrollViewer x:Name="SettingsScroll" VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto">
    <StackPanel x:Name="CardStack" MaxWidth="1250" Margin="24,16,24,24">
      <TextBlock x:Name="PageTitle" Classes="tm-page-title" Margin="0,0,0,8" />
      <cards:AppearanceCard x:Name="Appearance" />
      <cards:BehaviorCard x:Name="Behavior" />
      <cards:FoldersCard x:Name="Folders" />
      <cards:ToolsCard x:Name="Tools" />
      <cards:UpdatesCard x:Name="Updates" />
      <!-- 关于 is last, and it is the only group with no interactive control — Round 5, item 2 moved it
           here from the rail. See AboutCard.axaml for why the page ends on identity. -->
      <cards:AboutCard x:Name="About" />
    </StackPanel>
  </ScrollViewer>
</UserControl>
